From 07b416d899d8d9e7c6786f61a4c85dbcbe0eef63 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Micha=C5=82=20Iwanicki?= <michal.iwanicki@3mdeb.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 22:40:34 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] x86/slaunch: release the PSP TMR once the AMD IOMMU is
 enabled
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

On a PSP-assisted SKINIT launch the bootloader sets up a Trusted Memory
Region over the memory it launches into, and SKL leaves it in place for
the DLME to release once its own DMA protection is up. Xen has no PSP
code, so the TMR stays active for the whole boot. On the Gigabyte
MZ33-AR1 no interrupt then reaches the CPU: every delivery path fails
and Xen panics in check_timer() with "IO-APIC + timer doesn't work!".

Add a client for the PSP DRTM mailbox. It reads the PSP BAR out of the
System Management Network through registers 0xB8 and 0xBC of device
0:0.0, maps C2PMSG_72, and issues DRTM_CMD_TMR_RELEASE for TMR 0, the
only TMR the bootloader sets up. The argument registers C2PMSG_93
through C2PMSG_95 stay unmapped because this command takes no argument.

Whether the launch went through the PSP is decided by walking
configuration space for an AMD Secure Processor, against the same device
list the bootloader and SKL match on, rejecting the entries tagged as
carrying no mailbox as they do. A match means a TMR is live, because the
bootloader sets one up whenever its own probe succeeds and aborts the
launch if that setup fails. Reading psp_version out of the SLRT AMD info
entry would be cheaper but does not work: the bootloader fills that field
in grub_skl_prepare_bootloader_data() while staging the table, and its
own grub_psp_discover() runs later from dl_entry(), so the field is
always zero.

The release runs at the end of iov_detect(), which both the x2APIC path
and the plain path reach, through amd_iommu_init_late() and
amd_iommu_init(false) respectively. Three properties of that point
matter. Every IOMMU is enabled against a programmed device table.
early_time_init() has calibrated the TSC, without which __udelay()
computes a tick budget of zero and the mailbox handshake polls a few
microseconds instead of a few seconds. And setup_IO_APIC() is still
ahead, so check_timer() runs with the TMR gone. Linux places the same
call in amd_iommu_enable() for the first of those reasons.

A TMR that stays in place ends the boot either way, so a failed release
panics naming that as the cause, and a command that does not land prints
C2PMSG_72 so the failure says whether the PSP is still working, has gone
quiet, or is not answering at all. The one case that is not fatal is a
zero BAR, which is where the bootloader also gives up and launches
without a TMR.

The handshake allows the five seconds SKL does rather than the 400ms
Linux does, SKL being the implementation known to drive this mailbox.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Michał Iwanicki <michal.iwanicki@3mdeb.com>
---
 xen/arch/x86/Makefile                       |   1 +
 xen/arch/x86/amd_psp.c                      | 309 ++++++++++++++++++++
 xen/arch/x86/include/asm/amd_psp.h          |  69 +++++
 xen/drivers/passthrough/amd/pci_amd_iommu.c |  20 ++
 4 files changed, 399 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 xen/arch/x86/amd_psp.c
 create mode 100644 xen/arch/x86/include/asm/amd_psp.h

diff --git a/xen/arch/x86/Makefile b/xen/arch/x86/Makefile
index ce2e80cc80..9c1ae07410 100644
--- a/xen/arch/x86/Makefile
+++ b/xen/arch/x86/Makefile
@@ -57,6 +57,7 @@ obj-y += percpu.o
 obj-y += physdev.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_COMPAT) += x86_64/physdev.o
 obj-y += psr.o
+obj-y += amd_psp.o
 obj-y += intel_txt.o
